The following are the names of the 10 major royal temples in the Angkorian period: 1: Chinese temples
This temple used to hold the Shiva penis called "Jayesvara" to dedicate to Shiva or Iso, the deity who has power over all the gods. But now the Chinese temple remains only a laterite structure and a single stone throne that once housed the Shiva Jesvara.
2: Bakong Temple
Prasat Bakong is a royal temple in Hariharalaya, currently located in Rolous area, Prasat Bakong district, Siem Reap province. This magnificent temple is larger than the Chinese temple, which was built during the reign of King Indravarman I (877-889) in 881 AD to dedicate to Iso or Shiva, with the name Shiva. "Indrey Svara."
It should be informed that King Indravarman I was the son of Jayavarman III and the nephew of Jayavarman II. After succeeding his father, King Indravarman I moved the capital from Mahendrabhav to Roluos, naming the new capital "Hariharalaya".
4: Prasat Prang
It should be informed that in Yasodharapura, after the death of King Hasavarman I in 925, King Isanvarman II (925-928) continued to reign. As for Jayavarman IV, although he was initially a monarch, but after Isanvarman II died at Yasodharapura in 928 AD, he was married to a Brahmin priest and became a full-fledged king. Management rights over the bush.
5: The Transfiguration Temple
6: Takeo Temple
It should be informed that Takeo temple is currently located in Rohal village, Nokor Thom commune, Angkor Thom district, Siem Reap province, along Vong Touch road, which is the road to visit Angkor resort.
7: Phiman Akas Temple
8: Baphuon Temple
9: Angkor Wat temple
Bayon Temple was built during the reign of Jayavarman VII in the late 12th century, which is the royal temple of the third city of Yasodharapura, present-day Angkor Thom. This large structure was built to dedicate to the bodhisattva Avalokitesvara in Mahayana Buddhism, the religion of the state of Jayavarman VII.
Please be informed that Angkor Thom was the last capital in the Angkorian period, from the reign of King Jayavarman VII until the departure of Angkor in the early reign of King Srey Suryaporn II or King Ponhea Yat.
