(Phnom Penh): A senior official of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has urged those in power and influence not to intervene to release overweight trucks and seize them for a year.
Despite the opportunity, some car owners still refuse to wake up and continue to be aggressive, continue to be overweight without fear of the law and the road. Under the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, from September 30, 2021 to February 2022, 136 overweight vehicles were found and seized. Store for one year.
Mr. Ek Rath pointed out that the operation to crack down on overweight trucks encountered many difficulties, such as the driver and the car owner did not cooperate, drove the car and ran away. In addition, there are threats of throwing cars, hitting car windows and so on.
Apart from these problems, what is even more difficult is the attempt to intervene with the powerful and influential to release the overweight truck for various reasons, which the Ministry is not able to do.
"I do not want to reveal my name, but all of them are big people so as not to hurt their reputation. We just know that when we start there is always an intervention. The interventionist is the big one. "But the intervention was not successful, but he still intervened, making the team difficult." This is the confirmation of Mr. Ek Rath.
Due to the above reasons, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has made efforts to prevent overweight transportation.
"The Ministry of Public Works and Transport, especially Senior Minister , has taken care to crack down on the crime of overweight trucks because when we are overweight, our roads deteriorate quickly," he said. Pre-life, pre-technical breakdown. "This is why his leadership urges the team to crack down on overweight trucks."
The Undersecretary of State in charge of cracking down on overcrowding crimes called on all transport operators to respect the law, do not overweight by law to participate in the maintenance of roads that the Royal Government has spent a lot of money. Lots to build.
He also called on powerful people not to seek intervention to release overweight trucks, which is illegal and tantamount to engaging in road-destroying activities.
Despite many obstacles and difficulties, Mr. Ek Rath asserted that both the leadership of the Ministry, especially Senior Minister Sun Chanthol and the working group to crack down on all overweight transport offenses did not give up and continue to crack down. Carrying this weight to protect the road for a long time to avoid premature damage.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has always called on the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to strictly enforce this overweight truck.
