Working group to resolve the dispute between the people and the owner of Borey High Tech in Chbar Ampov
(Phnom Penh): Many families living around the construction site of Borey High Tech Champa Kbal Koh, located along National Road 1 in Chroy Ampil Village, Sangkat Kbal Koh, Khan Chbar Ampov, Phnom Penh, have been affected by the floods for months. Crops flood homes and make it difficult to live.
Starting from the implementation of this high-tech Borey construction project, the flood-affected people around the above Borey site have applied for intervention from the Sangkat / Khan authorities to solve the flood problem for them by attaching the thumbprint of 40 families.
After receiving a request for intervention from the people, the district authorities summoned the company and the people to settle at the district hall, and even made minutes of the meeting. The first solution between the company and the people does not seem to get the positive results that the company promised the people.
In fact, on the morning of February 23, 2022, the Dispute Resolution Team together with the Office of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Land Administration went to the construction site to inspect the site, with the participation of the company and the people's representatives.
Residents affected by the floods claim that the floods were not natural floods but man-made floods, which were caused by dredging. Pour the owner of Borey High Tech Champa Kbal Koh without leaving the waterway for drainage.
The villagers asked the district authorities to help solve the problem with the company, asking the company to install a drainage system to release water from the village in accordance with the technical standards of the construction and to install a pump for pumping water out when there is flooding. In addition, ask the company to pay compensation for any damage to their crops.
Regarding the request of the people, the company representative has not yet commented, saying that he is busy making notes with the authorities.
