General knowledge: When did Mr. Chiv Ta Kwan come to Angkor?
* Mission in Cambodia
From the beginning, the Chinese used to call Cambodia "Chenla". To strengthen relations with Chenla, Emperor Timur Khan of the Yuan Dynasty ordered Chiv Ta Kwan to visit Yasodharapura (present-day Angkor Thom) as the Chinese ambassador to the Yuan dynasty.
During his stay in Angkor Thom, the Chinese ambassador was allowed to bow to King Sindravarman or Indravarman III during July 1297 for a court hearing with the King.
In addition, he visited in and around Angkor Thom, writing notes describing the capital, palaces and temples, as well as the traditions and customs of the people of Angkor. He observed the procession of kings and ceremonies, as well as the daily lives of the Cambodian people. For Mr. Chiv Ta Kwan's stay, he asked to stay with a family near the northern gate of Angkor Thom, the current pottery gate.
After visiting Cambodia for 11 months, in July 1297, Chiv Ta Kwan left Cambodia to return to Beijing to report to his emperor. What is noteworthy is that he wrote handwritten notes on Cambodia.
Chiv Ta Kwan's document was translated into Khmer by Ly Theam Teng, a Cambodian scholar, in 1973 as an important document for Cambodians to understand Chinese records. .
