Japan condemns Russia for recognizing Ukraine as "independent state"
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Japanese Foreign Minister Yoshimasa Hayashi on Tuesday condemned Russia for recognizing two pro-Moscow separatist areas in eastern Ukraine and saying Tokyo plans to take action. Coordinate with other countries to impose sanctions against Russia. This was released by CNN on Tuesday, February 22, 2022.
"Our country will monitor the situation with great concern and will work with the G7 countries and the international community to respond decisively, including with sanctions," Hayashi told reporters. . Also on Tuesday, the Japanese Embassy in Kiev issued a statement on its website urging all Japanese citizens in Ukraine to leave immediately.
Russian President Vladimir Putin on Monday announced the recognition of two pro-Russian separatist areas in eastern Ukraine, the Donetsk People's Republic (DNR) and the Luhansk People's Republic (LNR). It also ordered the deployment of Russian troops to the two areas under the peacekeeping framework.
The United States, Britain and its Western allies have also condemned the recognition of the two breakaway states, saying it violates international law. But Russia claims that the declaration of recognition and deployment of peacekeepers to the two regions is only to prevent bloodshed and to protect its citizens.
