International Analysis: Has a diplomatic settlement with Russia reached a dead end?
(Moscow): For those watching the Ukraine crisis, you probably already know that February 20 is the date that the United States predicts when Russia will act. The 2022 Beijing Winter Olympics closed on Sunday, with reports claiming that Russian President Vladimir Putin would order his army to launch an attack on Ukraine.
Such predictions are not baseless. That is, based on Russian military action near the Ukrainian border and the history that has happened. Russia invaded Georgia in 2008 during the Beijing Summer Olympics and gradually occupied Crimea on the last day of the 2014 Olympics in Sochi.
Meanwhile, Russian-backed rebels in eastern Ukraine have deployed high alert forces and temporarily evacuated civilians to Russian territory amid reports of a ceasefire violation. So does this mean that the diplomatic settlement has failed?
Clearly, it is not all. On Sunday, Putin and French President Francois Hollande held telephone talks, and the two leaders agreed on the need to maintain an open dialogue at all levels regarding the situation in Ukraine. According to the Kremlin, compliance with the Minsk Agreement is the only way to ease the situation.
But the Ukrainian government sees the deal as compulsory under the threat of gunfire.
Recent satellite imagery by US technology company MAXAR shows that Russia has just deployed more armored vehicles and troops on the border with Ukraine, in line with a warning from the foreign minister. The United States says Russian troops are preparing to invade Ukraine soon.
