(Phnom Penh): On the morning of Monday, February 21, 2022, the Air Force Command held a meeting to review the work achieved in 2021 and set the direction for further work for 2022.
The meeting was chaired by General Soeng Samnang, Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander of the Air Force Command. The meeting was also attended by commanders and commanders, as well as military officers from various units under the Air Force Command.
In this morning's meeting, the commanders from each unit were given the opportunity to present the achievements of the unit as well as highlight some of the challenges that the unit faces to submit to the management to find a solution. .
Lt. Gen. Kong Mony, Deputy Commander of the Royal Thai Air Force, highlighted the achievements of the Royal Thai Air Force in 2021 and highlighted some of the Air Force's strengths and challenges.
After listening to the reports, General Soeng Samnang, Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander of the Air Force Command, expressed his appreciation and appreciation for the responsible attitude of the leaders of all institutions and Commander-in-Chief, Deputy Commander-in-Chief, Special Forces of all local units under the command of the Air Force Command, who worked hard to accomplish all the tasks in the old year.
General Soeng Samnang also gave an interview to the media. In this interview, General Soeng Samnang revealed that there are two core tasks that the Air Force has focused on, including human resource training and participation in the fight against Kovid 19.
Regarding human resource training, General Soeng Samnang emphasized that the Royal Government seeks scholarships in friendly countries in flight and technical skills. In addition, the command also runs local training, using officers who have received training abroad to help train locals.
As a follow-up direction for 2022, General Soeng Samnang has shown his efforts to train more human resources in the air force and to consider providing more technical equipment to keep up with the evolving situation of the world. King issues royal decree to rehabilitate three former CNRP leaders
