National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking Holds Consultative Workshop on Procedures and Procedures for Identifying Trafficking Victims to Provide Appropriate Services
(Phnom Penh): The National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking on February 21, 2022, held a workshop to consult, review and revise the handbook on the procedures and procedures for identifying victims of human trafficking. To provide appropriate services.
The workshop was chaired by Mr. Nhek Vannara, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, and was attended by a number of stakeholders at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
On behalf of Minister Vong Soth, he stated that the Guidelines on the Procedures and Procedures for the Identification of Victims of Trafficking in Persons to Provide Appropriate Services have been compiled since 2015 for six years. Therefore, it is time to revise the content of the principles or add new ones to the current social context and make it clear to use common procedures.
The Secretary of State said that due to the past, the data has not been divided on the identification of victims or vulnerable people because the practices of the relevant ministries, institutions and organizations are different, inconsistent or inconsistent. Which makes it difficult for front-line officials to implement, especially the report is not consistent.
In this regard, the senior official suggested that there should be a single mechanism for both ministries, institutions and partner organizations to implement in a consistent manner and send all that data to a national institution as a manager and would like to send a message to you. Please join the discussion and provide good input to make this book more comprehensive, easy to understand and more effective.
