(Phnom Penh): Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, said that the Royal Government will not be able to reduce the price of fuel tax because it will affect revenue and the national budget. For economic support.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth's remarks came after the Royal Government was asked to reduce the price of fuel tax, while now the retail price of fuel at each station has increased to more than 5,000 Riels for Super Gasoline, 4,700 Riels for Regular gasoline and 4,450 riel for diesel.
In a public forum on "Macroeconomic Management and Budget Law 2022" organized by the Ministry of Economy and Finance on 18 February 2022, Mr. Vongsey Visoth said that the increase in fuel prices is a common trend that affects all. Each. At the same time, the state has facilitated some things, such as setting a fixed tax rate even if the price of oil imported from international markets increases or decreases.
At the same time, the Ministry of Commerce monitors retail sales every month to determine the price of fuel sold at each station for regular gasoline and diesel to avoid any fraud.
He claimed that a reduction in the tax on gasoline would affect national income to a large extent.
"We understand the difficulties of the poor business people," he said. But we do not lack income, only oil-related businesses, we lack income to support the millions of poor people we are supporting, now we are helping the poor about $ 30 million a month This is a policy option "It is difficult to believe that in the current situation, reducing the tax on gasoline is not a better option than supporting the poor and vulnerable."
At the same time, officials representing the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia confirmed that although the price of goods in the international market increased, but the state did not consider raising the world market price, but taxed at a fixed price.
In 2021, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia collected tax revenue from the import of Tel Seila (fuel) products amounting to 2,157.1 billion Riels, equivalent to approximately 250 million dollars.
