On that occasion, Mr. Keo Remy said that the establishment of the National Human Rights Institution (NHRI) in Cambodia has been discussed for decades, and Cambodia has received a series of recommendations from treaties and institutions. From UN member states through the Universal Periodic Review Mechanism.
In September 2006, Cambodia took a strong stance in establishing a national human rights body in Cambodia, assigning the NGO working group responsible for drafting the law on the establishment of this human rights body.
Since August 2019, the Cambodian Human Rights Committee (CCHR) of the Royal Government has been tasked with drafting the Law on the Establishment of the Cambodian Human Rights Institution in collaboration with the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees. OHCHR and the Asia-Pacific Forum (APF) based in Australia.
Since July 2021 ADCC Launched and disseminated and consulted on the draft law to the public and with stakeholders such as NGOs, civil society, unions, political parties and relevant ministries and institutions in order to gather views, opinions and other input on the draft law to Ensure that this law is in line with the Paris Principles, the international minimum standards for the establishment of the NHR.
