BEIJING: China will work with Asian countries to increase the use of local currency in trade and investment as part of a plan to strengthen regional economic resilience. According to Reuters.
Reuters quoted Yi Gang, governor of the People's Bank of China, as saying at a G20 event, "Progress over the years has been made by developing countries in Asia in the use of local currencies in trade." Karma and investment have strengthened the region's financial safety net against external problems.
"Developing countries should boost their resilience," Yi said in a video at an event hosted by Indonesia. "This is something that regional cooperation has an important role to play."
He added that the bilateral currency exchange among ASEAN countries plus China, Japan and South Korea reached $ 380 billion.
Last month, the People's Bank of China extended a two-year bilateral currency swap agreement with the Central Bank of Indonesia to deepen financial cooperation and boost investment.
Yi added that the central banks of emerging economies should continue to strengthen market ties, as doing so would reduce the spread of influence in developing countries at increased risk of the Kovid-19 pandemic. According to Reuters, tomorrow, the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit will hold a press conference on "Local Progress of Pailin Commune and the Continuing Work Direction".
