Cambodian, Japanese Army Chiefs Pledge to Strengthen Ties Together and Plan to Hold Joint Exercises in the Future
The meeting was held on the occasion of the official visit of a high-level delegation of the Cambodian Army for 4 days from 14 to 17 February 2022 in Japan to celebrate the 30th anniversary of peacekeeping operations. PKO) of Cambodia in Cambodia, the first overseas operation of the Japanese Self-Defense Forces within the framework of the United Nations.
In this meeting, General YOSHIDA Yoshihide welcomed the arrival of Lt. Gen. Hun Manet and the Army delegation in Japan, despite the circumstances of Kovid-19, as a special opportunity for Discuss the strengthening of bilateral relations and cooperation between the Cambodian Army and the Japanese Army Self-Defense Forces.
He also highlighted the continued support for the Cambodian Armed Forces in capacity building through training in key skills to respond to non-traditional war security situations, such as cyber-attacks and modern technology, disasters and Epidemics, as well as support for joint exercise to some extent in the future.
On that occasion, the General also thanked Cambodia as the first country in Southeast Asia to support Japan's "Indo-Pacific Free and Open Strategies Initiative (FOIP)" for peace, stability and development. Joint development and prosperity in the region and globally.
In response, Lt. Gen. Hun Manet recalled the good relations and cooperation between the two countries and thanked the Royal Government, the Japanese Self-Defense Forces and the Japanese people for always supporting Cambodia in the peace process. Rebuilding the country after the war and up to the present stage, which is the stage of developing the country to glory.
In particular, he emphasized the significant progress of cooperation in all fields, including the military, since 2013, the year of the launch of the strategic partnership between the two governments, as well as the year of Military cooperation and exchanges between the two countries' Ministries of Defense.
At the same time, the Deputy Commander of the Army also thanked the Japanese side for always assisting the Cambodian Army in training human resources by providing long-term scholarships for active duty officers and commanders. Staff and so on.
It should be noted that this is the first official visit abroad by the Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces after the situation of Kovid-19 has improved, which is a further historical link between the two armies. Both the mainland and the two countries after the official visit of General YUASA Goro Commander of the Japanese Land Self-Defense Forces in Cambodia in 2020.
