Experts in agriculture, economy and development partners work together on sustainable agriculture
(Siem Reap): Transforming Cambodia's agriculture into a high-energy, high-growth sector is crucial for our country to accelerate recovery, poverty reduction and environmental growth.
Chan Serey Ratha, Country Director of the Cambodian Partnership for Sustainable Agriculture (CPSA), strongly believes that bringing key stakeholders together in a single forum is crucial to building this progress.
These points were achieved at the 2022 Meeting on Sustainable Agriculture co-organized by Amru Rice, the Cambodian Agricultural Corporation (CACC) and the Cambodian Partnership for Sustainable Agriculture (CPSA) on 10 February. 2022 at Angkor Howard, Siem Reap.
The meeting was attended by representatives from the agricultural community, banks, microfinance, input suppliers, service providers, agricultural machinery suppliers, NGO partners, development partners, donors, agricultural business leaders, government agencies and stakeholders. 200 people.
The meeting noted that during the Covid-19 pandemic, the transformation of Cambodia's agricultural and food ecosystems was increasingly important to ensure a strong food chain, affordable and nutritious food, and A vibrant rural economy.
This event is unique in promoting equality between the interests of business, people and the environment by sharing the Buddhist philosophy of Venerable Kou Sopheap and Dr. Heng Mony Chenda, the importance of walking the middle path and giving to each other Come to the land that we have used and enjoyed to achieve sustainability and harmony.
Dr. Mey Kalyan, Senior Advisor of the Supreme National Economic Council (SNEC) and Chairman of the Board of the Agricultural Corporation of Cambodia (CACC), stated, “Modernization of Cambodia's agricultural sector is an important agenda for our country. "The transition from low-income countries to middle-income countries must be achieved through the effective transformation of agricultural ecosystems that are responsible, professional and environmental."
"Our vision is to become a food secure, resilient Cambodia with thriving farmers and agribusiness in partnership with the government," said Peng Trida, director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
"Achieving this vision requires careful efforts among stakeholders in the fisheries sector, led by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, to continue to empower agricultural entrepreneurs and the private sector to "Increase agricultural productivity and profitability by taking into account sustainability, resilience and protection of ecosystems and biodiversity."
The meeting fully supported the "new thinking" of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in agricultural development.
Several proposals shifted the focus from specific crops to improving the overall resilience, competitiveness and sustainability of rural areas by launching ecosystems and protecting biodiversity.
Global experience shows that while ensuring access to input is still important, the focus of key public expenditures on public investment, including research and development (R&D), infrastructure, innovation, and market information systems. And biosafety will be able to better reduce poverty and increase productivity through general modernization of agriculture.
The smallholder farmers who attended the meeting on sustainable agriculture said that there is still a need for the farming community, the agribusiness sector and the government to improve measures so that smallholder farmers can have better access to inputs for financing. Invest and market for their products, while agribusiness enterprises and wholesalers emphasize that manufacturers should ensure the quantity and quality of products for timely processing.
The meeting called for interventions through community farming projects, better network services, e-commerce and investment in agribusiness start-ups that could help boost Cambodia's agricultural modernization.
Song Saran, CEO of Amuroi and President of the Rice Federation, said: “These models of change are very important in responding to emerging local and global market opportunities, while also helping "Create jobs, increase farmers' incomes and ensure Cambodia's food security needs and respond to the new challenges of climate change."
Pheasant Sophoan, Director of Oxfam, said: “Sustainable agriculture, smart farming technology, smart agriculture and green financing play an important role in reducing poverty and social inequality in Cambodia, especially for women working in the chain. Agricultural supply. Oxfam welcomes the cooperation of the private and public sectors present at the Sustainable Agriculture Summit. "
