Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Co-Chairs of the World Peace Meeting 2022 Sign the Joint Statement of the 11-Point Meeting
The 2022 World Peace Summit also marked the release of a joint statement on the search for peace on the Korean Peninsula.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Ms. Hak Jahan Moon, Founder of the Global Peace Federation and Mr. Ban Ki-moon, Former Secretary-General of the United Nations, Co-Chairs of this Important Summit, Jointly Sign the Joint Statement Well.
The three-day 2022 World Peace Summit on the Search for Peace on the Korean Peninsula discussed and gathered views from former leaders from 157 countries. The meeting is a global effort to end the 72-year-old separatist conflict on the Korean Peninsula.
Through in-depth discussions, current and former heads of state, government, parliamentarians, senior representatives, religious leaders, academics, business leaders and the first women journalists, women leaders and artists who attended the World Peace Summit Decided to issue 11 joint statements as follows:
1. Urge the two Koreas and the international community to seek bilateral and multilateral diplomatic mechanisms to avoid military conflicts, strengthen stability and predictability, and enhance mutual understanding and mutual trust to build Achieving peace on the Korean Peninsula without nuclear and complete peace.
2. Call on all parties concerned to continue constructive discussions with mutual respect and recognition of each other's security interests and concerns based on the common cultural heritage of the two Koreas.
3. Promote confidence-building measures, confidence-building programs and diplomatic protection involving key stakeholders working on the Korean Peninsula.
4. Call on all parties concerned to formally end the "war mindset" and work together to support President Moon Jae-in's call for an end to the Korean War, opening a new page for a culture of peace and non-violence.
5. Propose proposals and forums for constructive dialogue and consultation based on the spirit of friendship and brotherhood that serve the interests of the Korean people and build a path to a peace treaty on the Korean Peninsula.
6. Find ways to turn the Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) into a peace park through the deployment of non-military guards and the development of cultural corridors and joint tourism projects to promote common prosperity in both Koreas.
7. Encourage stakeholders to establish cooperation projects in tourism, sports, culture and education in North Korea and other countries.
8. Establish multi-sectoral cooperation to promote food security and public health cooperation, especially between the Korean Peninsula and the Mekong region and other countries through the Mekong Peace Park Project.
9. Work towards the normalization of relations between the two Koreas to allow the people to get the maximum benefit from the invaluable potential that both states have to offer, especially in the fields of politics, economy, culture, humanitarian affairs. Health care, education, society (youth and sports) and infrastructure.
10. Strengthen multilateral dialogue and cooperation on cultural and digital linkages and innovation to use the international community's important role in promoting peace, stability and prosperity on the Korean Peninsula.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, winner of the 2022 Sun Hak Peace Prize, was one of the keynote speakers invited by the General Assembly to share ideas for resolving the Korean Peninsula issue.
At the General Assembly, Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the break-up of the Korean Peninsula was a legacy of the Cold War and that the Korean people were victims of the politics of the superpowers. Unfortunately, the superpowers are selfish and use the Korean Peninsula as a buffer zone to challenge and predict their power.
Samdech Techo drew attention to the General Assembly that peace and security in the Asia-Pacific region and the world as a whole are inseparable from peace and security on the Korean Peninsula, and peace and security on the Korean Peninsula are inseparable from peace and security. Security in the Asia-Pacific region and the world as a whole is due to the fact that the worlds are interconnected and interconnected.
As the founder of peace, the leader who ended the long civil war in Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also spoke about the history of the division and suffering of Cambodia because of the war. Samdech Techo told the General Assembly that the Cambodian people have experienced the pain of war when they went through nearly three decades of civil war, which occurred mostly due to foreign intervention.
