Samdech Techo Hun Sen drew the attention of the General Assembly that for decades, peace efforts for the Korean Peninsula have been carried out at all levels and forums, but unfortunately to no avail. Peace and stability on the Korean Peninsula remain ambiguous and fragile due to previous unsuccessful approaches that require courage and innovative ways that can yield positive results.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen further stated that the disintegration of the Korean Peninsula is a legacy of the Cold War and that the Korean people are the victims of the politics of superpowers. Unfortunately, the superpowers are selfish and use the Korean Peninsula as a buffer zone to challenge and predict their power.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen demanded the consideration of three issues to find peace on the Korean Peninsula. The three issues to consider are:
First, overcoming more than 70 years of turmoil and mistrust requires years of negotiations and confidence-building measures, and, of course, a gradual reconciliation process. Likewise, the denuclearization process will take place in stages. Therefore, strategic patience is paramount.
Third, building trust steps requires regular, participatory participation from peace negotiators, who should focus on efforts that can yield quick, non-controversial, non-sensitive and practical results.
In his keynote address to contribute to resolving the Korean Peninsula conflict, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also proposed the initiative "Two States Towards One Nation: One Peninsula, One People, One Culture" as a concept. Towards the end of the war or tensions on the Korean Peninsula.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Asks Leaders from Various Fields to Provide Elements in the Initiative "Two States Towards One Nation: One Peninsula, One People, One Culture" as a Concept Document in Action Practical and achievable. Samdech Techo stressed that trust can be built based on actions, not promises.
