(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, requested the Government of Canada to examine the possibility of establishing a free trade agreement with Cambodia to promote trade and investment between Cambodia and Canada.
Mr. Pan Sosak also mentioned the significant increase in bilateral trade between Cambodia and Canada, in which Cambodia exported to Canada under the free tariff program and free quota of up to 954 $ 82 million in 2021.
Minister Pan Sosak also said that Cambodia has always supported Canadian investors who are investing in Cambodia. In fact, ABA Bank ranks third among the leading banks in Cambodia, and Manulife Insurance Company has also been licensed and shared its expertise to Cambodia.
At the same time, Mr. Pan Sosak requested the Canadian side to continue the assistance under the CTIF project on technical assistance to Cambodia in relation to the development of SMEs capacity, participation in exhibitions and matching for trade and investment meetings between the two countries. There are special preferences for Cambodia, which has a smaller economic gap than some ASEAN countries.
In response, the Ambassador expressed his deep gratitude to the Minister of Commerce and praised Cambodia's good relations with Canada and Cambodia with ASEAN, and replied that this is a special year for Cambodia to be President of the ASEAN. Coinciding with the 45th anniversary of ASEAN-Canada relations.
At the same time, the Ambassador expressed her deep appreciation to the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, who achieved the planned vaccination campaign for many people and praised the efforts. Cambodia's efforts to lead ASEAN this year, despite the difficult times of the Kovid-19 crisis.
At the end of the meeting, Minister Pan Sosak expressed Cambodia's commitment to enhancing good cooperation between the two countries and the efforts of the Ministry of Commerce to help improve the business environment in Cambodia. In particular, to facilitate Canadian businesses to do business in Cambodia.
