Cambodia experienced a devastating fire crisis for nearly four decades, from March 18, 1970 to December 29, 1998, the day set for the end of the war after the military and Khmer Rouge leaders agree to live with the Royal Government under a win-win policy set by Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
At the opening ceremony of the World Peace Meeting held in Seoul, South Korea, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also shared the history of the division of Cambodian society with the former leaders of the country as well as guests. Invited from around the world.
The Prime Minister reminded that the war in Cambodia could not end despite international intervention, especially the intervention of the United Nations. Samdech Techo Hun Sen pointed out that the United Nations has spent more than 2 billion dollars, but can not end the flames of war in Cambodia. But in the end, the flames of the Cambodian war were extinguished under negotiations under a settlement between Cambodians and Cambodians through win-win politics.
In the presence of leaders, former leaders and relevant organizations from about 157 countries, Samdech Techo stressed that "in more than half of my life, the negotiation process is the best option. I never believed in using war to end a war. "Our country is torn by war. We have used four years of negotiations to end this war."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the complete peace that Cambodia achieved at the end of 1998 was the peace and national unity of Cambodia that has never been seen in the history of Cambodia for at least the last 500 years.
The end of the war under this win-win policy has made Samdech Techo highly respected and admired for his skillful ideas. Astonishment exists not only in the Cambodian community, but also in the regional and international community.
