Pen Socheat, undersecretary of state and exporter of the Ministry of Commerce, said the reason there were no exporters under the two agreements was because some exporters needed to study the market and diversify their products. Do the export.
At the same time, because the exporters continue to use the ASEAN-China trade agreement, most also get the same zero tariff rate on existing goods. And for new products, exporters are studying more markets.
He said that currently, traders and exporters are in contact with partners in the countries to be imported under the framework of the two agreements, while the import of goods from China to Cambodia has already made the framework of this agreement.
"Utilizing the benefits of the RCEP is based on efforts to diversify new products that benefit from both the RCEP and the free trade agreement with China," he explained. "Exporters need a little more time to study the market, which is different from some Chinese exporters who have already prepared some goods that have already been brought to Cambodia under this agreement."
The Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement and the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) entered into force on January 1, 2022. The export markets under the RCEP are 15 countries, including the 10 ASEAN members and five free trade partners: Australia, China, Japan, South Korea and New Zealand.
Mr. Pen Socheat said that the Ministry is currently promoting new products for the market under the Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement and the RCEP market by preparing a handbook for producers to benefit from. Free trade agreements with China, Korea and the RCEP agreement, which are export-oriented, benefit from these agreements.
The Ministry of Commerce expects that in the next two or three months or at the latest by the first half of this year, Cambodia may achieve its goal of exporting some goods to other markets under the benefits of the agreement. Both of these.
