The following is the decision of the Ministry of Interior: BREAKING: The court issued arrest warrants for 3 former NagaWorld employees in connection with the obstruction of the implementation of measures to prevent Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): Three former NagaWorld employees have been remanded in custody by Phnom Penh Municipal Court Investigating Judge Ouk Reth Kunthea in connection with the obstruction of the implementation of measures to prevent 19 diseases.
It should be noted that the police force of Phnom Penh Municipal Police arrested the three accused on the night of February 5, 2022 on charges related to obstructing the implementation of measures to prevent Kovid 19 disease.
According to the court's detention order obtained by Fresh News on the night of February 9, 2022, the three former NagaWorld employees were charged by Deputy Prosecutor Seng Heang of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court. "Obstruct the implementation of measures."
The three former NagaWorld employees who were arrested and detained include:
* First name: Chuob Channath, male, 44 years old, living in Sangkat Tonle Bassac, Khan Chamkar Mon * Second name: Seng Vannarith, male, living in Sangkat Or Russey II, Khan 7 Makara * Third name: Sao Sambath, living in Phum Chrey Kok, Sangkat Choam Chao 2, Khan Por Sen Chey They were arrested by police at 7:30 on February 5, 2022.
The three were arrested in accordance with the arrest warrant issued by Deputy Prosecutor Seng Heang of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court to question the obstruction of the implementation of measures to prevent Kovid 19 under Article 11 of the Anti-Corruption Law. Kovid's disease 19.
Meanwhile, the Phnom Penh Municipal Police is looking to bring in four more former NagaWorld employees. The search for the arrest was carried out in accordance with an arrest warrant issued by the Phnom Penh Municipal Court in connection with a case that prevented the implementation of measures to prevent Kovid 19 under Article 11 of the Anti-Kovid Law.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sok Chenda Sophea, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, Secretary-General of the Council for the Development of Cambodia, said that in contributing to building a green ASEAN and a resilient future with a sustainable environment, Cambodia will strive to increase environmental sustainability and address issues. Climate change and encourage investors to focus on environmental protection and biodiversity conservation.
Speaking on the topic of ensuring green rehabilitation for a resilient ASEAN, Mr. Sok Chenda Sophea outlined a number of practical measures that the Royal Government of Cambodia has put in place, including a long-term development strategy. By carbon neutrality and the strategic framework and programs to restore and stimulate Cambodia's economic growth in living with Kovid-19 in the new normal trajectory based on three pillars (3Rs).
He added that for the resilience pillar, the Royal Government will strengthen its readiness and response to ensure socio-economic development by focusing on human resource development, small and medium enterprise development, including resilience to change. Climate and the promotion of green economic development as well.
He also mentioned the new investment law, which came into force on October 15, 2021, which states that 19 sectors and investment activities are encouraged in These include investments in environmental protection and conservation, biodiversity and chakra economy, as well as investments in green energy, technologies that contribute to climate change adaptation and mitigation.
The OECD Southeast Asia Program is co-chaired by Korea and Thailand in the second phase, and for the next phase, Australia and Vietnam will co-chair for four years.
Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth told the Minister of Agriculture that "the lack of a transparent competition mechanism is partly leading to higher pork prices."
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth, Minister of Economy and Finance, told the Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries that the import of live pigs to supply the Cambodian market must be auctioned to ensure the import of live pigs. A competitive price keeps the price of live pigs and the selling price of pork in the market at a reasonable level.
In a letter to the Minister of Agriculture on February 8, obtained by Fresh News on February 9, Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth stated that the increase in the price of live pigs was partly due to a lack of competitive mechanisms. Between companies importing live pigs for supply to Cambodia.
According to the Deputy Prime Minister, research figures show that live pig importers licensed by the Ministry of Agriculture buy live pigs from Thailand for between 6,500 riel and 7,000 riel per kilogram on average. Resale in the country up to 11,000 riels on average in 2019 and 2020 and up to 14,000 riels on average in 2021.
To avoid the above phenomenon, Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth decided to set up a bidding mechanism for the import of live pigs to supply in Cambodia. The Deputy Prime Minister believes that this mechanism will help promote transparent competition in the business of importing live pigs, which contributes significantly to ensuring the stability of live pork prices and pork prices in the market.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Economic and Financial Policy Committee, said that the organization of the auction mechanism for importing live pigs to supply to Cambodia will enhance competition. Transparency in the business of importing live pigs, which will contribute significantly to ensuring the stability of live pigs and meat prices in the market.
The remarks came after the Ministry of Agriculture requested that the Ministry of Economy and Finance suspend initiatives on arranging live pig auction procedures for imports into Cambodia.
He asserted that the mechanism for bidding for the import of live pigs was to be used only when there was a shortage of domestic pigs, not to be implemented on a regular basis. At the same time, determining the amount of live pigs that will be allowed to be imported at any time is the responsibility of the bidding committee chaired by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Eligible private companies to apply for the tender to import live pigs are required to qualify in terms of technical, financial and other requirements. On the other hand, this auction uses the method of lower bidding, but the price of imported live pigs will be set to a certain level to ensure that it does not affect the price competition in the market of local farmers.
