Tensions on the Korean Peninsula have flared up again after Pyongyang resumed a series of missile tests, including ballistic missiles banned by the UN Security Council. In January, Kim Jong Un's regime tested seven missiles, including the supersonic yellow missile and the Hwasong-12 missile, capable of hitting US bases or territories in the Pacific. .
A statement issued by the North Korean Foreign Ministry on Tuesday said that a series of missile tests since New Year's Day have further demonstrated the progress of North Korea's military capabilities. Also mentioned is the ICBM Hwasong-15 intercontinental ballistic missile, which is believed to be capable of carrying nuclear warheads at the United States.
"Many countries today are wasting their time dealing with the United States in the face of blind obedience and obedience," the statement said. Only our country in the world can shake the world through missile tests that can hit anywhere in the United States. "There are nearly 200 countries in the world, but very few have hydrogen bombs, intercontinental ballistic missiles and supersonic missiles."
Since 2017, North Korea has not tested nuclear weapons or intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs), but past missile tests have raised concerns that Pyongyang may test these weapons. Repeat in the near future. Prosecutors decide to charge three former NagaWorld employees with "obstruction of action"
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Seng Heang, Deputy Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, on the morning of February 9, 2022, decided to charge three former NagaWorld employees for obstructing the implementation of measures in accordance with Article 11 of the Law. Fighting the spread of Kovid 19.
The three groups of protesters are: Chuob Channath, male, 44 years old, living in Sangkat Tonle Bassac, Khan Chamkar Morn, Second: Seng Vannarith, male, living in Sangkat Or Russey, Khan 7 Makara, and third: Sao Sambath, who He was arrested by police at 7:30 on February 5, 2022.
The three former NagaWorld employees were sent to court by the Phnom Penh Municipal Police on February 08, 2022, and after the indictment, the prosecution sent the three accused to the investigating judge. Proceed with the procedure.
Lt. Col. San Sok Seiha, spokesman for the Phnom Penh Municipal Police, confirmed to Fresh News that the arrests were made on a warrant issued by Deputy Prosecutor Seng Heang. First, Phnom Penh to inquire about the prevention of the implementation of measures to prevent Kovid 19 according to Article 11 of the Kovid 19 Fight Law.
Pursuant to Article 11 of the 199 Kovid Disease Control Act, which states that obstruction of the implementation of measures stipulates that: Intentional obstruction or obstruction of the implementation of measures issued in accordance with this law shall be punishable by imprisonment for a term of six months. To 5 years and a fine from 2,000,000 Riels to 10,000,000 Riels.
The article also states that the offender shall be sentenced to imprisonment from 2 to 5 years and a fine from 5,000,000 to 20,000,000 Riels if the above act causes the transmission of Kovid-19 disease to Others or seriously affect public health.
On the night of February 7, the Phnom Penh Municipal Health Department announced that it had just discovered 19 positive cases of Kovid virus in eight more former NagaWorld employees, while more than 30 were found to be infected. Contained Kovid-19 virus during previous demonstrations.
Two law professors prepare textbook "General Criminal Law" to help students study
(Phnom Penh): Two law professors, Mr. Run Ponnarith and Ms. Chang Sinath, have jointly created a special work by compiling a textbook on "General Criminal Law" to help law students.
The textbook "General Criminal Law" has the following benefits:
1: Understand the general theory of criminal law, focusing on the elements of crime, criminal responsibility and punishment.
2: Understanding how to write in response to various questions, especially case studies.
3: There are questions for training and answers to strengthen knowledge.
This book is important for law students who wish to compete in the legal profession and those who wish to study and understand criminal law. Cambodian Entrepreneurs Partners with Dutch Ministry of Commerce to Bring Cambodian Handicrafts to International Markets
(Phnom Penh): Cambodian Entrepreneurs (KE) under the Ministry of Economy and Finance have signed a strategic cooperation agreement with the Center for the Promotion of Imports (CBI) on behalf of the Ministry of Foreign Trade and Development Cooperation. The Netherlands.
Both institutions will help local SMEs in home decoration and home textiles connect with European and global markets.
Dr. Chheang Vann Monin, Executive Director of Cambodian Entrepreneurs, stated, "In line with the Royal Government of Cambodia's policy on diversifying exports and quality Cambodian handicrafts, this collaborative project will introduce Cambodian products to international markets that will bring benefits. "Many such as supporting existing businesses and opening up new markets for small and medium enterprises in the handicraft sector, which has been hit hard by the Kovid-19 crisis."
In this joint project, Cambodian entrepreneurs will provide financial support to selected SMEs to help them implement market readiness activities. CBI will provide coaching, training and marketing support. Small and medium enterprises will participate in training and guidance sessions, which will be led by CBI trainers with many years of experience.
Dr. Chheang Vann Monin added that improving access to global markets is important as the program will help small and medium enterprises expand their business operations. Pauline notes that in addition to providing direct support to small and medium enterprises selected for this partnership, it also helps develop a business environment for home decoration and home textiles in Cambodia.
This joint project will run until 2024. Cambodian entrepreneurs and CBI will participate in other activities to improve market access and help Cambodian SMEs become more competitive.
