(Phnom Penh): The director of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Department of Water Resources, Mr. Yim Bun Rom, responded with a slap in the face to the Minister of Agriculture, claiming that water resources are not just about irrigating rice and giving up thinking about other agriculture. In that, even the water supply for people's consumption is considered in terms of water resources.
Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries Veng Sakhon is often seen trying to use public forums to attack the Ministry of Water Resources, saying it has never considered setting up a water supply system for vegetable crops. Just think about irrigating rice.
Despite several attacks on the Ministry of Water Resources, Minister of Water Resources Lim Kean Hor was as cold as ice, never retaliating against his former Minister of Agriculture.
Although the Ministry did not respond to the questions of the media in the press conference on the progress of the Commune / Sangkat of Banteay Meanchey Province organized by the Royal Government on Tuesday morning, February 08, 2022, Mr. Yim Bun Rom responded and clarified. On this issue as well.
Citing the Banteay Meanchey provincial framework, for example, Mr. Yim Bunrom claimed that the Ministry of Water Resources as well as the Department of Water Resources in Banteay Meanchey province had taken into account all aspects of this water supply, including irrigation of rice and agriculture and animal husbandry. Including the consumption of the people.
Mr. Yim Bun Rom said that in Banteay Meanchey province, in addition to the construction of large canals, the department also has the construction of small canals to irrigate other crops.
Mr. Yim Bun Rom confirmed that "the water resources sector is not just about rice. "In short, from cattle, from plantations, from alternative crops, from industrial crops, from rice, from daily consumption," he said.
Please note that in Banteay Meanchey province, the irrigation system that provides the ability to irrigate 87 small and large. It is divided into 6 large-scale systems, 75 medium-scale systems and 6 small-scale systems, with large-scale irrigation from 1,000 hectares up, medium-irrigation below 5,000 hectares and small-scale irrigation from 500 hectares down.
