Reuters reports that US President Joe Biden last weekend extended a four-year tariff on solar energy imports, despite easing some conditions by removing the double-sided solar panels. The house is capable of generating energy on both sides and is used extensively by large projects in the United States.
Former United States President Donald Trump has lifted tariffs on Chinese solar panels imports in 2018, exercising his rights under Article 201 of the 1974 Trade Act. The tax deduction started at 30 percent and dropped to 15 percent.
"The US government is committed to continuing to implement the measures under Article 201, despite strong opposition from stakeholders at home and abroad, and the continued exclusion of these tariffs," the Commerce Department said in a statement. "Not only did it not help the development of American industry in the country, but it also undermined the normal order of international trade in the field of solar panels, a new energy product."
China has expressed hope that the United States will take strong steps to facilitate free trade in new energy products and "contribute to the development of the world in a low-emission economy."
Due to rising overseas protectionism and rising domestic demand, Chinese solar panel manufacturers have become increasingly focused on the domestic market. According to Reuters.
