President of Japan-Cambodia Association promises to submit Cambodia-Japan free trade proposal to Japanese government
The desire for free trade between Cambodia and Japan was stated by Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, in a working meeting with Mr. Fumiaki TAKAHASHI on February 3, 2022. The talks are aimed at strengthening cooperation between the two countries and promoting a better business environment.
The Minister of Commerce added that Japanese investors have come to invest in Cambodia, bringing the flow of technology, knowledge and skills from Japan Direct Investment (FDI) and Official Development Assistance (ODA). In addition, the Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement between the Association of Southeast Asian Nations and Japan (AJCEP) has accelerated the response to economic development between Cambodia and Japan through the provision of free trade, services and investment.
According to the minister, Cambodia also imports about 400 to 500 tons of raw silk from Japan per year to produce handicraft products for export to major markets, including Thailand, Vietnam, China and Japan. The Japanese market will become Cambodia's largest silk import market by 2020, valued at up to $ 1.5 million.
The Minister called on Japanese businessmen, entrepreneurs and investors to consider Cambodia as a business partner and investment destination as the Royal Government of Cambodia has ensured stability, peace and politics, as well as a favorable investment and business environment for Japanese investors.
In response, the President of the Cambodia-Japan Association expressed his appreciation and appreciation for the Royal Government of Cambodia's efforts to ensure peace and social stability, especially the achievement of the Kovid-19 vaccination campaign for the people. Nationwide.
At the meeting, in addition to promising to bring the free trade proposal to the Government of Japan, the President of the Association also expressed support for the development of silk price chain investment in the target provinces and continue to support the investment of Japanese entrepreneurs in Cambodia.
