(Phnom Penh): The accident rate is still at an alarmingly high rate caused by driving without respecting traffic laws. Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, asked all civil servants to set an example of respecting traffic laws, driving and educating relatives to reduce the rate of traffic accidents.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol made the call during a ceremony and handed over more than a thousand helmets to officials of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport who travel by motorbike.
During the ceremony, Ms. Mon Manvi, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary-General of the National Road Safety Committee, emphasized that traffic accidents occurred alarmingly after the Royal Government reopened the country after the Kovid 19 crisis.
She pointed out that in 100 accidents, 70% of motorcyclists and 75 people do not wear a helmet. Causes of traffic accidents are due to speeding, disrespecting the right of way, risking turning and overtaking, not being right-handed, drunk, technical, drowsy and drowsy.
According to a study by Development Partners, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that currently Cambodia's losses due to traffic accidents have increased between 420 to 450 million US dollars per year.
To help reduce the rate of traffic accidents and reduce road tragedies, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol called on all government officials, especially officials of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, to set an example of obeying traffic laws and educating their families. Relatives and friends to respect traffic laws together.
The Senior Minister appealed, "Please join and be an example to our people." If we ride a motorbike, we are officials of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, lawmakers must obey the law. In particular, our car has a license plate number 25. Journalists like to take pictures just to see the license plate number 25 and 25 of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport. He posted that the lawmakers do not respect the law.
The senior minister also called on the leadership of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to instruct its drivers to obey traffic laws and be extremely careful when driving to set an example for the people.
