US orders embassy staff and their families to leave Belarus
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States on Monday ordered its embassy staff and their families to leave Belarus as Americans were warned not to travel to a Russian ally. This too. This is according to the release by the Chinese newspaper CGTN on Tuesday evening, February 1, 2022.
"Given the concerns and irregularities of the Russian military near the Ukrainian border, Americans who are in Belarus or planning to travel to the country should do so," the statement said. "Be wary of unforeseen situations and escalating regional tensions."
The issuance of the order by Washington comes as tensions between it and Moscow over Ukraine continue to rise. Hundreds of thousands of Russian troops and heavy weapons are also known to be present on Russian waters near the Ukrainian border, as the West continues to accuse Russia of invading Ukraine as soon as mid-February.
Thailand's Samet Island tourism could lose more than $ 45 million due to oil spill
(Bangkok): Tourism on the island of Samet, Thailand may lose more than 1.5 billion baht (about $ 45 million) due to the recent oil spill into the sea, affecting travel confidence in the next three months as operators Tourism urges the government to take strict preventive measures to protect natural resources. According to the Bangkok Post.
At the moment, there are very few long-stay tourists who came to rest before the oil spill.
The Bangkok Post has no new bookings from local tourists, despite Chinese New Year. Meanwhile, international tourists under the Test & Go project have postponed their travels or decided to choose other destinations.
The risk is high because between January and April is a high holiday season for the local market.
Travel confidence will take no less than one to three months to recover, as tourists are concerned about health issues related to oil and chemicals from the surface oil refining process.
"Tourism operators struggling to recover from the outbreak have high hopes for the market in February," said Sarinthip. "But the spill made our expectations for a short time disappear."
