Ministry of Environment Introduces Measures to Prevent and Reduce Public Air Pollution to Ensure Better Air Quality
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, the air quality is still below the standard of good quality, although the inertia particle concentration level has increased, especially the PM10 and PM2.5 inert particle parameters. Anyway.
The Secretary of State stated that according to the results of air quality monitoring from air quality monitoring stations installed by the Ministry of Environment in capitals and provinces across the country, the air quality situation has decreased, especially the concentration of inert particle parameters. PM10 and PM2.5 may rise above the standard in some provinces, posing a risk to human health and the environment.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the main sources and activities that cause the PM10 and PM2.5 inertia particles to increase, including emissions from industrial plants from diesel vehicles from various fuels, in particular. Forest fires, burning of grass, burning of agricultural waste, burning of forest, burning of rice stumps, burning of solid waste in the open, and burning of landfills, as well as dust from construction sites.
The Secretary of State stated that by examining such issues and to improve air quality in the capital-province, the Ministry of Environment informed the sub-national administration and the capital-provincial environment department the following measures:
1. Take measures to prevent wildfires and burning of solid waste in the open, fires in landfills. 2. Take measures to prevent dust from construction sites and infrastructure improvements and dust on the roads. Roads 3: Cleaning the streets and public places. 4: Educate people not to burn garbage, solid waste, burning grass, stumps, walls and other agricultural waste.
He said that as a measure to reduce air pollution, the Ministry of Environment has initiated a clean air plan for Cambodia with technical and financial cooperation from the United Nations Environment Program's Asia-Pacific Clean Air Partnership and the Environment Institute. Com of Sweden.
Recently, the Ministry of Environment released the Cambodia Clean Air Plan, a national strategic document aimed at reducing air pollution emissions in Cambodia.
The Cambodia Clean Air Plan is designed to integrate with the policies and regulations of the Royal Government of Cambodia, such as Circular No. 01 on Measures to Prevent and Reduce Public Air Pollution, Sub-Decree on the Control of Air Pollution and Noise Introduced as a measure to reduce air emissions.
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment called on the sub-national administration and the Capital-Provincial Department of Environment to take effective and responsible measures to maintain good air quality for the people of Cambodia. Chinese New Year
