"Sustainability of Cambodia Landscape and Ecotourism" Project Receives $ 57.58 Million to Participate in Ecotourism Development
(Kampong Speu - Koh Kong): Ecotourism is a tour of natural areas rich in ecosystems. Hiking, hiking, camping or hammocking, watching the stars at night in the woods away from the city, listening to the cries of animals. These are all activities that can be done by visiting eco-tourism sites throughout the country.
Organizing and promoting ecotourism not only can create job opportunities and income for local people, it can also contribute to the effective conservation of natural resources. Local people who receive income from tourism activities generally stop migrating and stop all acts of destroying natural resources, such as deforestation and killing wildlife, to keep for tourism.
He said that the project "Sustainability of Cambodia's landscape and eco-tourism" has a scope of implementation in seven provinces, including the provinces around the Tonle Sap Lake and Kampong Speu and Koh Kong. Covering 14 natural resource protection areas and 2 landscapes around the Tonle Sap Lake and the Cardamom Mountains.
With the receipt of these funds, the project manager stated that most of the funds are used to create an environment for investment from the private sector, such as the construction of roads, clean water, sanitation, especially the strengthening of ecotourism destinations and natural communities. .
According to the project official of the Ministry of Rural Development, there are two types of road construction projects in ecotourism areas, including concrete roads or two-story rubber roads.
Recognizing this need, the "Sustainable Landscape and Ecotourism of Cambodia" project will take six years to implement (2020-2025) under the World Bank credit line.
Visiting ecotourism sites makes tourists understand and respect natural resources. And starting from this respect, they will participate in the protection of natural resources on their own, without any call or encouragement from anyone.
Of course, the design and promotion of ecotourism sites alone cannot completely protect natural resources. Protecting natural resources is a huge and complex task that requires the participation of all stakeholders. But at least once you get to know Cambodia's rich ecotourism sites, the will of tourists to protect their natural resources will surely increase.
During the tour, participants will travel to various areas in Kampong Speu and Koh Kong provinces, such as Chambok Ecotourism Community, Suramarit Kosomak Kirirom National Park, Chi Phat Ecotourism Community, Thmarong Cave, Sintamani Valley Camping Area. Cardamom Mountain, Zoo Station near Chi Phat, Stung Tatai, River 4, Floating Islands, Areng River, Phnom Mrek Kang Kep and Stung Pongrol Waterfall.
