"Chambok", a charcoal kiln that deforestes nearly 1 million cubic meters a year, has become an attractive eco-tourism site
(Kampong Speu): Chambok Ecotourism Community was established in 2002 with financial and technical support by Mlup Baitong and the cooperation of community forestry and conservation community, geographically located in Chambok Dangkum Village, Chambok Commune, Phnom Sruoch District. Kampong Speu province has a road distance of about 110 kilometers. After 2009, the community managed to independently manage its community.
Tourists can travel from Phnom Penh along National Road No. 4 at km 185, right on the reed, the entrance to Kirirom Road is about 20 km, at the foot of Kirirom Mountain, turn right on the red gravel road for about 8 km. Will reach Chambok ecotourism community.
In the past, to get to the waterfall, tourists had to take a bullock cart or motodup for about 2 kilometers, but now tourists can travel by car directly to the Chambok Community-Based Tourism Office. Then travel uphill for many kilometers to reach many places of entertainment at different distances.
If you look at before the establishment of Chambok ecotourism community, this natural site is rich in more than 400 charcoal kilns, which cut down nearly 1 million cubic meters of forest per year. But since the establishment of Chambok ecotourism community, the area no longer has coal mines.
"The community covers a total area of 3,451 hectares, with 637 families registered as members. In this community, in addition to taking visitors to visit the natural areas in the community, they are engaged in farming, non-timber forest products, and some of them go to work in the mango plantation, jackfruit, in addition, he also seeks tourism services. This is according to the additional confirmation of the community leader.
The community leader added: "If we look at the currents of change, it seems that on the day of the first community, there were more than 400 kilns in the case study for more than a year. One million cubic meters is a lot, we first studied, so at the beginning of 2002, 2008, the furnace began to decline until now there is no coal incinerator.
There are resources for tourism such as: traditional dance, visit Anlong Roth, plant seedlings, make souvenirs, make a car, make ambok, visit the vegetable garden with the villagers, visit Chambok waterfall, visit rice fields, forests, waterfalls, bats and camps. The community also provides services such as: restaurants, restaurants, guides, accommodation, bicycle rental, motorbike rental, car rental, bulldozer rental.
