(Phnom Penh): Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, this morning, January 27, 2022, confirmed the activities of an organization that ranks corruption in Cambodia as a bad country. Neighbors, contrary to what is true in Cambodian society today, and the Royal Government of Cambodia does not care about this ranking.
In a press conference on "Local Progress, Communes and Continuing Ratanakkiri Province", Mr. Phay Siphan claimed that the activities of Transparency International Cambodia, a non-governmental organization, were merely puppets of foreigners, while the classification did not reflect the law. A clear way in public for the public to hear.
Mr. Phay Siphan stated, "The Royal Government of Cambodia does not value in the rankings. It is said that Cambodia is inferior to Burma, which is ridiculous Not a representation of Cambodia's efforts. "I do not accept what this announcement is."
The government spokesman said that what he considered to be a joke was that when the organization held an evaluation forum, it invited government representatives to participate in the program's schedule and provided an opportunity to express their views. In fact, when the program runs, they do it alone, while the government representatives are invited to participate, just to sit and listen like the clock.
In addition to reacting indiscriminately to the ranking of this foreign mercenary organization, Mr. Phay Siphan, through a conference forum attended by a large number of journalists, also highlighted the actions and efforts of the Royal Government. Some Cambodians to fight corruption as well. It also includes an interview with Senior Minister Om Yentieng, head of the Anti-Corruption Unit, outlining Cambodia's actions.
Mr. Phay Siphan added that Cambodia is not only willing to fight corruption, but also to cooperate with the United Nations and to follow the rules properly.
According to Phay Siphan, what the Royal Government of Cambodia has done to combat corruption includes establishing a one-stop shop at the local level to serve the people without receiving unofficial money, and establishing an anti-corruption law. Corruption and access to information laws. All of which are mechanisms to combat corruption in Cambodia.
Through the Anti-Corruption Law, the Anti-Corruption Unit is open to action without having to ask for a warrant from the prosecutor's office, that is, if corruption is found, action can be taken immediately. This is according to what Mr. Phay Siphan highlighted.
He stressed that Transparency International Cambodia is just one of those who, according to their reports, do not know how far Cambodia has come to fight corruption.
