Thai regulator plans to ban the use of digital currency to pay for goods and services
(Bangkok): Regulators in Thailand plan to ban the use of digital assets to pay for goods and services because it could pose a risk to financial and economic stability. According to the Bangkok Post.
Ms Charuphan added that the public hearing began on January 25 and is scheduled to run until February, after which regulators are expected to jointly decide and finalize the regulations.
According to SEC documents related to public hearings on these regulations, licensed digital asset operators have expanded their activities to show that they are ready to support businesses and stores in using digital assets for work. Payment.
The rules apply to brokers and traders, digital exchanges, cryptocurrencies and digital casinos, as well as digital property advisory providers, the Bangkok Post reported.
These regulations state that these businesses must not be eager or show that they are ready to support stores in using digital assets for payment of goods and services, or provide a system for stores to facilitate No payment.
Businesses under the regulations are not allowed to open e-wallets for businesses to receive digital asset payments.
The SEC said the regulations came into force from the date of this notice.
