SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Singapore's Foreign Ministry has issued a high-level directive banning its citizens from traveling to Ukraine as the situation there escalates. This was released by CNA on Wednesday evening, January 26, 2022.
"The current situation is on the rise in tensions in both Ukraine and the surrounding region," a statement from Singapore's foreign ministry said. The Singapore Ministry of Foreign Affairs has also strongly advised its citizens already living in Ukraine to remain vigilant and monitor the situation closely.
Singapore does not have a diplomatic mission in Ukraine, so the announcement is aimed at reminding its citizens who are there to try to maintain their own security.
The West has warned since late 2021 that Russia could attack Ukraine as hundreds of thousands of troops and heavy weapons are already deployed near the Ukrainian border, despite Russia's response. Many times it has said that it only gathers troops and weapons to protect its own safety.
