On January 26, 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen met again with General Ming Oung Lang on video to exchange information and developments related to Myanmar issues.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Minister accompanying the Prime Minister, told the media after the meeting that in addition to exchanging information, Samdech Techo Hun Sen submitted four requests to Mr. Ong Lang to be implemented. The light of day to resolve the crisis in Myanmar.
1. Samdech Techo calls on Mr. Minh Ong Lang to work hard to implement the five principles of ASEAN unanimity set out in April 2021 at the ASEAN Leaders' Special Meeting in Indonesia.
2: Samdech Techo requested General Minh Ong Lang to make good efforts to pave the way for the special envoy of the ASEAN Chair to visit Myanmar in the future.
3. Samdech Techo calls on all parties to the conflict in Myanmar, including the ruling party, to stop all violence and reach a ceasefire in the future. In this regard, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, as the chair of ASEAN, expressed concern over the violence following his visit to Myanmar in early January. Samdech Techo asked all parties to show patience.
4: Samdech Techo invited General Minh Ong Lang to cooperate with ASEAN countries in providing humanitarian assistance to the most needy people, as mentioned by Samdech during his meeting in Nay Pyi Taw.
Myanmar has been facing a political crisis in the country since February 1, 2021, after the military arrested and imprisoned Aung San Suu Kyi after her party won the election.
The crisis has dragged on for about a year, until Myanmar did not attend ASEAN meetings in 2021. Myanmar's absence from ASEAN was considered by Samdech Techo Hun Sen as a split in ASEAN.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, as the chair of ASEAN, expressed his strong intention to solve Myanmar's problems, especially to bring Myanmar to the ASEAN forum. At the beginning of January 2022, just became the chair of ASEAN, Samdech Techo Hun Sen traveled to Myanmar.
In response to Cambodia's request, Myanmar's military leader Aung Ming Lan agreed to postpone the shootings with relevant parties in the country. The military leader agreed to facilitate the special envoy of the ASEAN chair to hold talks with the parties to the conflict in Myanmar and to facilitate the provision of assistance to the people of Myanmar on all sides.
The results of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's visit are seen as a step and a light in resolving the crisis in Myanmar.
In addition to his recent trips to Myanmar, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has met with leaders of ASEAN member states to discuss Myanmar's issues.
Through that meeting, the leaders of ASEAN countries all supported the efforts of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, however, they still demanded that the Myanmar military regime fully implement the five points of unanimity adopted by ASEAN, otherwise Myanmar military leader Ming Aung Lan should not be invited to the ASEAN summit.
In a meeting with the Prime Minister of Malaysia on January 25, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also reiterated his position that if the implementation of the five principles does not progress, Cambodia will not invite Ming Ong Lang to the ASEAN Summit. And assisted by non-political parties.
Today's meeting with Ming Ong Laing demonstrates Samdech Techo Hun Sen's commitment and efforts to find a solution for the ASEAN member Myanmar, even though the issue is seen as very complicated. Anyway.
ASEAN Tourism Association suggests ASEAN should expand ATCAF implementation of tourist travel
(Phnom Penh): The ASEAN Tourism Association (ASEANTA) has suggested that through the framework of the ASEAN Agreement for Corridor Travel (ATCAF), ASEAN member countries should expand the scope of this agreement to cover tourist travel to facilitate In addition to traveling within ASEAN, as well as taking advantage of key market sources outside ASEAN.
In that message, the President of the ASEAN Tourism Association was pleased to note and support the remarks made by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, which promotes intra-ASEAN travel and the opening of borders for tourism from ASEAN countries. Other major markets.
The President of the ASEAN Tourism Association suggested that the ASEAN Framework Agreement on Corridor Travel (ATCAF) should be extended to leisure and short-distance travel to further facilitate intra-ASEAN travel. "As well as taking advantage of key market sources outside of ASEAN, especially countries that are ready to launch their tourism."
According to the President of the ASEAN Tourism Association during the tenure of the ASEAN Chairmanship for 2022, Cambodia should open discussions on this issue in order to achieve success in restoring tourism in the ASEAN region.
The ASEANTA President added that in order to further support this initiative, an interdisciplinary digital health program is needed to ensure that international travel in ASEAN is more convenient and secure.
