(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment, with the technical and financial support of the United Nations Environment Program's Asia-Pacific Clean Air Partnership and the Stockholm Environmental Institute of Sweden, aims to reduce emissions in the country. Cambodia has implemented a Clean Air Plan in Cambodia to prevent air pollution that adversely affects public health, the environment and economic development.
He added that the Cambodian Clean Air Plan is designed to integrate with the policies and regulations of the Royal Government of Cambodia, such as Circular No. 01 on measures to prevent and reduce public air pollution, sub-decree on air pollution control and Noise disturbance to put out as a measure to reduce air pollution emissions.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that the Cambodia Clean Air Plan is designed for the main purposes, including:
* First, to develop a joint analysis of pollutants, greenhouse gases and short-term pollutants to identify areas that are the main sources of air pollution now and in the future.
* Second, identify measures to reduce emissions and pollution in existing plans and strategies in line with reducing greenhouse gas emissions.
* Third: Identify other mitigation measures that can be implemented to reduce emissions better.
* Fourth: Identify the benefits of mitigation measures identified for improving air quality and reducing climate change.
* Fifth: Prioritize activities and pave the way for air quality management
* Sixth: Promote the planned implementation of existing plans and new activities being developed in various fields.
The governor of Kampong Chhnang province pointed out that the more than 7,000 people returned the land to the Royal Government at the request of Samdech Techo Hun Sen voluntarily and without any clashes with the slightest violence. He said the purpose of reclaiming the flooded forest land was to restore the natural resources of the Tonle Sap Lake.
Mr. Sun Sovanrith said, "The people who came to self-declaration so far are 7531 people, covering an area of 30,589 hectares."
He added that the Tonle Sap Lake does not belong exclusively to Kampong Chhnang or the surrounding provinces, that is, the Tonle Sap Lake belongs to all Cambodians, so if it continues to be destroyed by a powerful person. That clutch will destroy what is the common property of the Cambodian people, especially the habitat of all kinds of fish living in the Tonle Sap Lake.
According to the provincial governor, after receiving the land, which is a voluntary handover from the people, the authorities are also in the process of measuring and demarcating the border in two or three areas more clearly so as not to confuse the people. He said that experts are currently measuring the carpet, but not completely because the water has not receded.
He said that in addition to measuring the border, the authorities have also replanted some trees, including more than 80,000 palm seedlings, a large part of the support of Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Management. Land Urban Planning and Construction with Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration. Mr. Sun Sovanrith stated that the replanting of trees is the right planting in order to attract part of the tourism sector.
Therefore, he claimed that people who used to enjoy rice on the land they illegally occupied could also turn to the benefits of fish trade in the Tonle Sap Lake area and tourism.
At the same time, Mr. Sun Sovanrith pointed out that in the operation to remove the flooded forest from illegal occupation, the police also removed 55 illegal lands and removed 203 border posts. However, no other people were arrested except for two people arrested by the Anti-Corruption Unit.
Brigadier General Su Socheat and Colonel Keo Narun were arrested by the Anti-Corruption Unit after being summoned for questioning on December 16, 2021 in connection with the occupation of flooded forest in three areas in Kampong Leng district, Kampong Chhnang province. Prohibited areas of deforestation.
It should be noted that recently, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, issued a strict order to the ministries, institutions and provincial authorities, especially the provinces around the Tonle Sap Lake to launch a hot operation to crack down and confiscate flooded forest land. Who have been abused and occupied thousands of hectares in the past, especially in Kampong Chhnang province.
In connection with the flooded forest land, three district governors in Kampong Chhnang province, including Boribo, Kampong Leng and Choul Kiri districts, were also exchanged by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia.
