Rising gas prices and high inflation are worrying Asian countries
BEIJING (Reuters) - Rising gasoline prices are creating new challenges for governments as they try to keep their economy, which has been hit by the Kovid outbreak, back to normal.
In Asia, South Korea has taken a series of steps to ensure a stable supply of fuel while monitoring prices, and Japan may soon be able to provide assistance to local refineries, Bloombeg reports. India, Asia's second-largest oil consumer, lowered tariffs on gasoline and diesel during elections in some key provinces.
Retail inflation in India rose in December, the third straight month of growth, and further impacts are likely. According to a Bloomberg News survey, consumer prices in the country this year are expected to be the highest among the world's 10 largest economies. China, South Korea and Japan are at the bottom of the inflation list.
In Japan, higher energy prices are hurting household budgets, with inflation expected to be the highest since 2008, in part due to higher gasoline prices. South Korea's central bank is also worried about rising prices as inflation rose 3 percent in December for the third straight month. Indonesia, meanwhile, is tackling the issue of rising subsidy costs amid rising fuel prices.
Faced with this, South Korea cut fuel taxes in October as part of efforts to curb inflation. Since then, domestic gasoline has been declining for nine weeks before stagnating.
